=> Is HashMap thread-safe?
    -> No, HashMap is NOT thread-safe.
    -> Multiple threads can access and modify it simultaneously.
    -> Since there is no synchronization mechanism, concurrent modifications can lead to inconsistent results.
    -> It is suitable only for single-threaded environments.
    -> If multiple threads need to access a HashMap, additional synchronization is required.

=> What problems occur if multiple threads modify a HashMap?
    -> Multiple threads may update the same data simultaneously.
    -> This can lead to:
        -> Data corruption.
        -> Lost updates.
        -> Inconsistent data.
        -> Unexpected behavior during iteration.
        -> ConcurrentModificationException (while iterating and modifying in fail-fast scenarios).
    -> In older Java versions, concurrent resizing (rehashing) could even lead to an infinite loop.
    -> Interview Tip:
        -> HashMap is safe for multiple readers only if no thread performs write operations.

=> Why can HashMap lead to data corruption?
    -> HashMap does not use any synchronization.
    -> Two or more threads may modify the same bucket at the same time.
    -> Since updates are not coordinated:
        -> One thread's changes may overwrite another thread's changes.
        -> Internal bucket structure may become inconsistent.
        -> Some entries may become inaccessible.
        -> Data may be lost.
    -> This is why HashMap should never be used for concurrent write operations.

=> What should be used instead of HashMap in a multithreaded environment?
    -> It depends on the use case.

    -> Hashtable
        -> Thread-safe.
        -> Synchronizes every operation.
        -> Locks the entire table.
        -> Slower under high concurrency.

    -> ConcurrentHashMap (Recommended)
        -> Thread-safe.
        -> Better performance than Hashtable.
        -> Uses fine-grained synchronization and CAS (Compare-And-Swap).
        -> Allows multiple threads to read and write concurrently.
        -> Does not allow null keys or null values.

    -> Collections.synchronizedMap()
        -> Wraps an existing HashMap.
        -> Makes all operations synchronized.
        -> Suitable when concurrency is low.

    -> Interview Tip:
        -> For modern Java applications, prefer ConcurrentHashMap over Hashtable because it provides better scalability and performance in multithreaded environments.

=> Why does ConcurrentHashMap not allow null?
    -> ConcurrentHashMap does not allow null keys or null values.
    -> In a multithreaded environment, null creates ambiguity.
        -> `map.get(key)` returning `null` can mean:
            -> The key does not exist.
            -> The value is actually null.
    -> During concurrent updates, JVM cannot distinguish between these two cases.
    -> Therefore, ConcurrentHashMap completely disallows null keys and values.

=> Why is ConcurrentHashMap faster than Hashtable?
    -> ConcurrentHashMap does not lock the entire map.
    -> It uses:
        -> Fine-grained synchronization.
        -> CAS (Compare-And-Swap) for many update operations.
    -> Multiple threads can work on different parts of the map simultaneously.
    -> Read operations are mostly lock-free.
    -> Only the portion being updated is synchronized.
    -> This significantly improves performance in multithreaded applications.

=> Why is Hashtable slower?
    -> Hashtable synchronizes every method.
    -> It locks the entire table for every read/write operation.
    -> Only one thread can access the Hashtable at a time.
    -> Even if two threads are accessing different buckets, they must wait.
    -> This creates high contention and reduces performance.

=> What is fine-grained locking?
    -> Fine-grained locking means locking only the required portion of a data structure instead of locking the entire structure.
    -> This allows multiple threads to work simultaneously on different portions.
    -> In ConcurrentHashMap:
        -> Only the bucket (or a very small portion) being modified is synchronized.
        -> Other buckets remain available for other threads.
    -> Result:
        -> Better concurrency.
        -> Better scalability.
        -> Higher performance.

=> What is CAS (Compare-And-Swap)?
    -> CAS stands for Compare-And-Swap.
    -> It is an atomic CPU operation used for thread-safe updates without locking.
    -> It compares the current value with an expected value.
    -> If both values are equal:
        -> It updates the value.
    -> Otherwise:
        -> The update fails.
        -> The thread retries or uses synchronization if required.
    -> CAS helps reduce locking and improves performance.

=> How does CAS work?
    -> Step 1:
        -> Read the current value.
    -> Step 2:
        -> Compare it with the expected value.
    -> Step 3:
        -> If they match:
            -> Update the value atomically.
    -> Step 4:
        -> If they don't match:
            -> Another thread has already modified the value.
            -> CAS fails.
            -> Retry or synchronize.

=> Explain CAS with an example.
    -> Suppose the current value is:
        -> count = 10

    -> Thread A:
        -> Compare: Is count = 10?
        -> Yes.
        -> Update count to 20.
        -> CAS succeeds.

    -> Thread B:
        -> Compare: Is count = 10?
        -> No (it is already 20).
        -> CAS fails.
        -> Thread B retries with the latest value.

    -> Result:
        -> No data corruption.
        -> No unnecessary locking.

=> When does ConcurrentHashMap use CAS?
    -> CAS is mainly used during write/update operations.
        -> put()
        -> replace()
        -> update operations
    -> ConcurrentHashMap first attempts to update using CAS.
    -> If CAS succeeds:
        -> No locking is required.
    -> If CAS fails due to contention:
        -> It falls back to synchronization for that small portion of the map.

=> Are reads lock-free in ConcurrentHashMap?
    -> Yes.
    -> Read operations (`get()`) are mostly lock-free.
    -> Multiple threads can read simultaneously.
    -> Reads do not use CAS because they do not modify data.
    -> This is one of the major reasons ConcurrentHashMap performs much better than Hashtable.

=> What happens if multiple threads try to update the same bucket?
    -> Contention occurs on that bucket.
    -> ConcurrentHashMap first attempts the update using CAS.
    -> If CAS succeeds:
        -> The update completes immediately.
    -> If CAS fails:
        -> The thread retries or synchronizes only that bucket.
    -> Other buckets remain accessible to other threads.
    -> The entire map is never locked.

=> What happens if multiple threads are only reading?
    -> Multiple threads can read concurrently.
    -> No locking is required.
    -> No CAS is required.
    -> Since read operations do not modify data, they do not interfere with each other.
    -> This makes read performance extremely fast.
    -> Interview Tip:
        -> Remember this one-liner:
            -> Reads are lock-free.
            -> Writes use CAS first, then synchronize only if necessary.   